# C bindings (Gforth)

Use only when the project already binds C libraries. Declare functions first (see manual §5.26.2), then call by name.

## Argument order

Push arguments **deepest first** — same order as C parameter list. Integers and pointers on the **data stack**; floats on the **FP stack**.

```forth
\ off_t lseek(int fd, off_t offset, int whence);
fd @ 0 SEEK_SET lseek -1 = if
  s" seek failed" type cr
then
```

Most C functions return a value — **`drop` it explicitly** if unused (Forth does not ignore returns like C often does).

## Wide types

If a C type may not fit one cell, declare the binding with **double cells** (e.g. `dlseek` for large `off_t`). Struct/union pass-by-value is not supported portably — use pointers.

## Variadic functions

No generic `printf` binding — declare one `cfunction` per argument pattern you need.

## Strings

C expects NUL-terminated strings; Forth uses `( c-addr u )`. Convert at the boundary with project helpers — see `forth-strings.mdc`. Do not pass ephemeral `s"` buffers to C that outlive the call unless copied.
